<FeatureFacts id="coach-programs" />

## Your programs, LogIt's and the community's

Coaching › Programs has three lists. **Yours** are the programs you made or copied, each with
how many clients have it. **LogIt** has LogIt's own programs, and **Community** has program packs
from the plugin directory. Those two are read-only: Start from a program shows one with its days,
and Copy to my programs makes it yours to change. A newer version of a pack never changes your
copy, or your clients'.

## Build a program

<HowTo id="studio.program" />

Each day is a list you can drag (or move with the arrow keys on its grip); a superset moves as one.
Click an exercise to open its settings right there. **+ Add to** the day opens one search over
exercises, cardio and mobility drills: pick one and Add, or pick several and add them one after
another, as a superset (two), tri-set (three) or giant set (four or more), or as a circuit.

A superset shows a header with its name ("Superset", "Tri-set", "Giant set" or yours) and how it
runs. It stays a superset, back to back with no timer, whatever its size; click the header to make
it a circuit, name it, or set the rest between exercises and after the round, the same settings
the client has in the workout. **+ Exercise** on the header adds to it, and ⋯ has Ungroup.

Each exercise says what it **measures**: weight × reps, reps (bodyweight), reps + added weight,
time (holds like a plank) or distance (carries), and whether left and right are logged apart. The
set fields follow it. Holds run as a timed hold on the client's phone and carries as distance.
Cardio can have intervals (work, easy, repeat) and an effort, shown on their phone as "Planned".

Changes save as you go, so leaving the page loses nothing. If the same program is open on another
device, changes to different things come across by themselves with a note; only when both changed
the same exercise does LogIt ask which to keep. Programs made before the builder whose weeks have
different exercises are still edited week by week.

## Circuits and blocks from plugins

**As a circuit** (in + Add) makes exercises done one after another, round after round: how many rounds, the
rest between exercises and between rounds, and reps or a time for each exercise. It moves up the
same every week, by a round every two weeks, or by each exercise's own rule. Clients run it
with the circuit timer on their phone.

**Plugin blocks** (in + Add) adds a block from a workout-block plugin you've added, like timed rounds of
rolling or hangboard hangs. Each plugin asks for its own settings, plus how many rounds (or
hangs) it starts with. Find more in Plugins › Workout blocks.

Circuits and plugin blocks need LogIt 1.1.0 or newer on the client's phone; older phones say
"Update LogIt to see this part" and run the rest of the day.

## When a program uses a plugin

A plugin block, or a rule from a plugin, runs on the client's phone, so they're asked first:
opening the program shows what the plugin is, what it can see (that block's entries, or their
sets of those exercises) and that it can't read anything else or go online. **Allow** adds it
for your programs only, even if they keep community plugins off. **Not now** leaves the block as
a note in their workout and the rule's exercises repeating last time's numbers; the rest of the
program works. Their Training tab tells you which: not asked yet, not allowed, or removed from
their phone.

## Progressions

A progression decides how the numbers move from week to week. Pick one for the whole program at
the top of the builder, and a different one for any exercise from its settings. LogIt Adaptive,
Linear, the 4-week wave and Fixed are built in. Programs › Progressions lists them all with what
each does in a deload week, and is where you make your own,
with the same builder lifters have on the phone: where the weight comes from (strength estimate,
last session or a wave), the rep range, reps left, the set layout, the step, what happens on a
stall, the number of sets and whether to hold in a calorie deficit. "What it would do" shows the
numbers on a client's lift, and "In words" is what clients see under "Why this weight?". Your
programs follow changes to a progression; clients' copies follow when you update them.

A client's copy can follow its own progression: ⋯ on their Training tab. Clients' phones work out
each week's numbers from their own lifts when they start the day.

## Swaps

What clients can swap to is in Programs › Swaps: any exercise, the same main muscles, or only the
swaps you list for each exercise. The same page has every exercise's swap list: adding a swap
there adds it to that exercise in every program it's in (the builder shows the same list under the
exercise). On the phone, after a swap they choose just today or for the rest
of the program, with a reason if they like; the rest of the program tells you, and later days start
with the swap. On their Training tab, Keep it changes their copy to match, and Put it back… puts the
original back and tells them. A message asking about a swap has "Swap an exercise in their
program…" on its menu, to change it in their copy straight from the conversation. Whatever your
rule, "Do something else today" is always there for them; it logs as a swap you can see.

## Every week

See every week shows what the rule plans for each exercise, week by week. On a client's copy the
numbers come from their own strength estimates, and weeks they've done show what they did; on your
own program the rule is said in words ("6–8 reps, from their estimate") until you pick a client
under Showing to preview it with their lifts.

- **LogIt Adaptive** picks the weight from their estimate for the reps, leaving two in the tank.
- **Linear** adds a rep a week through the range, then the step (+2.5 kg) and back to the bottom.
- **4-week wave** runs 5s, 3s and 5/3/1 off a training max, then a light week.
- **Fixed** keeps the numbers as you typed them.

## Week types

The Weeks strip in the builder shows each week's type; **Edit weeks** sets them. A week's change
is on top of what the progression works out:

- **Normal**: the progression decides.
- **Intro**: an easier start, load −10%.
- **Deload**: half the sets, same weights. The progression pauses, and the week after carries on
  from the week before.
- **Heavy**: load +5%, two fewer reps.
- **Test**: the last set is as many as they can; it sets the next estimate.
- **Find your weights**: they work up to the weights that fit; the progression pauses.
- **Your own**: a name, and ± sets, ± load %, ± reps, a last set as many as they can, and whether
  the progression pauses. Once made, it's offered for every week of the program.

The client's Home shows the week's type with one line on what it means. A progression from a
plugin is told the week's type; if it doesn't handle it, LogIt applies the change to its numbers.

Double-click a cell, or press Enter, to type over it, like `62.5 × 7`; it stays put whatever the
rule does, with a blue edge. Clear gives it back to the rule. Copy, paste and Fill to the end of
the block (up to the next week with a type) are on the cell's menu.

## Give it to a client

<HowTo id="studio.assign" />

From a program, or from a client's page, Assign gives one or several clients their own copy. It
starts when they tap Start on their phone, or on a date you choose. Someone already on a program
can switch now at week 1, or start the new one the day theirs ends. Gave someone the wrong program
a few weeks ago? **Replace, at week N** starts the right one on the date the wrong one did, as if
they'd been on it all along. Either way their logged sessions stay in their history as done, and
their strength estimates and records stay, so the new program plans from their real numbers. A program with a block from a plugin
says so: without the plugin on their phone, that block shows as a note and the rest of the day
works.

On a client's Training tab, their program is at the top: which week they're in, its rule, the
next week with a type, this week's days and what they've done, and any swaps. Their main lifts show
their best set this week, their strength estimate then and four weeks ago, and what the program
plans next time. ⋯ on the program has Change program…, Plan the next block… and Stop the
program.

## When you change a program clients have

Each client has their own copy, so changing your program doesn't change theirs. When copies are
behind yours, the builder shows **Update N clients…**: it says what you changed and lists each
client with the week they're on. Ticked copies take your changes; anything you changed for one
client stays as it is, and the list says so. Clients finishing within a week are left unticked.
Their start date, the week they're on and everything they've logged stay; the changes apply from
their next session. **Only the library** keeps the change to your program alone.

Copies made before LogIt kept track of what changed for each client are replaced by your version
the first time (start and logs kept); after that they update like the rest.

## The next block

In a program's last two weeks, the client's Training tab shows **Plan next block** (also on
Today, and in Ctrl K on the client). It shows how the block went: sessions done of those planned,
check-ins, their weight trend, each lift's estimate at the start and now, and any muscle whose
weekly sets stayed under the usual range. Then pick what's next:

- **Run it again from their new numbers**: the same program from a new start; the rules plan
  from today's estimates, and numbers you'd typed over are cleared.
- **Copy it and change things**: the same, then opened in the builder for them.
- **Something different**: pick another program.

Small changes for a rerun: one more set on each exercise for the muscles that were behind, a
lighter first week, and keeping their swaps. Choose the start date and length, and whether to send
them the block's numbers in a message.

<CanCant ids={["coach-programs"]} />
